WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.856
An API has to know who is calling, and what that caller may do.

00:00:04.856 --> 00:00:10.052
The OpenID Connect Token Validation feature checks the access tokens that clients send.

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:13.852
This video shows how it works, how to configure it, and how to

00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:15.640
protect your own endpoint.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.034
Endpoints that serve other applications authenticate through the API scheme.

00:00:25.034 --> 00:00:29.593
A client sends its access token with each request, and the scheme passes

00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:31.230
it to a token handler.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.036
The scheme has no token logic of its own.

00:00:35.036 --> 00:00:38.482
It forwards to the scheme named in its options.

00:00:38.482 --> 00:00:45.440
When no such scheme is registered, nobody is authenticated, and a challenge answers 401 Unauthorized.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:51.637
Enabling the Token Validation feature points the API scheme at the validation handler.

00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:55.190
The handler is registered when the settings are valid.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.210
A valid token then authenticates the request.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.514
Validation has to know who issues the tokens.

00:01:05.514 --> 00:01:11.783
The settings name either an authorization server tenant or an authority, and exactly one of them.

00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:15.800
A tenant is a separate site hosted by the same installation.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:21.713
With a tenant, Orchard Core reads the signing and encryption keys straight from

00:01:21.713 --> 00:01:24.871
that tenant, so it sends no discovery request.

00:01:24.871 --> 00:01:28.090
The audience is fixed, so you cannot enter one.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:34.097
With an authority, Orchard Core finds the keys by discovery, and the audience

00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:36.313
your API expects is required.

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:41.000
The OpenID Connect Metadata Address overrides the discovery address.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.460
Disable token type validation accepts tokens from servers that do not set the access token type.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.508
Another tenant adds a requirement.

00:01:50.508 --> 00:01:55.219
Its server needs a scope that lists this tenant in Additional resources, API

00:01:55.219 --> 00:01:59.184
audiences, or saving fails with No appropriate scope was found.

00:01:59.184 --> 00:02:03.550
A tenant whose server uses reference access tokens cannot be selected.

00:02:07.762 --> 00:02:10.474
Features are under Tools, Features.

00:02:10.474 --> 00:02:18.082
Search for token validation to find OpenID Connect Token Validation, listed under the OpenID Connect heading.

00:02:18.532 --> 00:02:21.410
Select Enable, and the row shows Enabled.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:27.288
The feature builds on OpenID Connect Core Services, which the Authorization Server already

00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:28.941
enabled on this site.

00:02:28.941 --> 00:02:34.391
Roles other than Administrator need the permission View and edit the OpenID Connect

00:02:34.391 --> 00:02:36.962
validation settings to use its page.

00:02:41.152 --> 00:02:45.409
The settings are under Settings, OpenID Connect, Token Validation.

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:49.962
A warning at the top says the website might be restarted when you save.

00:02:50.418 --> 00:02:56.096
This site also runs the OpenID Connect Authorization Server, so Authorization server tenant

00:02:56.096 --> 00:02:59.901
is already filled in with the name of this tenant, Default.

00:02:59.901 --> 00:03:02.828
Tokens issued here are validated against it.

00:03:03.303 --> 00:03:06.584
To name a remote server instead, clear the field.

00:03:06.584 --> 00:03:12.667
Authority, Audience, and Disable token type validation appear around the OpenID Connect Metadata

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.873
Address, which is always shown.

00:03:15.332 --> 00:03:19.271
Enter the address of the server, and save without an audience.

00:03:19.271 --> 00:03:24.832
The page refuses, with the message An audience must be set when configuring the authority.

00:03:25.296 --> 00:03:29.177
Enter the audience your API expects, and save again.

00:03:29.177 --> 00:03:34.196
A message reads OpenID validation configuration successfully updated.

00:03:34.655 --> 00:03:39.736
Saving checks the form of the values, and sends no request to the remote server.

00:03:39.736 --> 00:03:44.994
This site also runs the Authorization Server, so Orchard Core applies that server's

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:47.475
issuer and keys after the authority.

00:03:47.475 --> 00:03:50.665
A remote authority belongs on a site without it.

00:03:54.865 --> 00:04:00.895
Name the API scheme in the Authorize attribute, and the token handler authenticates the request.

00:04:00.895 --> 00:04:05.565
This class also carries AllowAnonymous, so the action makes the decision.

00:04:06.315 --> 00:04:12.393
The action asks the authorization service whether the user has the permission the endpoint needs.

00:04:12.393 --> 00:04:16.995
When the answer is no, it returns ChallengeOrForbid with the API scheme.

00:04:17.745 --> 00:04:21.246
ChallengeOrForbid answers according to who is calling.

00:04:21.246 --> 00:04:26.788
Without a valid token, the caller is challenged, and the client is asked to authenticate.

00:04:26.788 --> 00:04:30.755
With a valid token but no permission, the caller is forbidden.

00:04:34.955 --> 00:04:38.951
A recipe sets the same values with its validation settings step.

00:04:38.951 --> 00:04:43.918
Enable the feature first, because a step without a registered handler is skipped.

00:04:43.918 --> 00:04:46.715
Then set Tenant, or Authority and Audience.

00:04:47.465 --> 00:04:53.036
The page checks your values when you save, but the recipe step stores what it receives.

00:04:53.036 --> 00:04:58.368
Invalid values register no handler, so the API scheme authenticates nobody, and the

00:04:58.368 --> 00:04:59.925
site logs a warning.

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:04.316
That is the Token Validation feature:

00:05:04.316 --> 00:05:09.042
the API scheme gets a handler, and your settings decide whose tokens it trusts.

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:12.100
A separate video shows how a client gets a token.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.545
Thanks for watching.

